    "result_text": "The full suite passes \u2014 **277 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation. Since the acceptance test (`Cp16Tests`) reads the value via a **GET** after creating (`fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(...))`), the count must be persisted, not just returned in the create response.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`CountNamesakes.java`** (new function) \u2014 counts existing owners with the same last name (case-insensitive) via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, before the owner is saved, and stores it with `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. Because it runs before `SaveOwner`, the count reflects only pre-existing owners.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `countNamesakes` step into the pipeline (after `assignMembershipTier`, before `save`), governed by the same transaction.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, mirroring the existing `membershipNumber`/`customerCode` computed fields.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` int32 property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically by name (both create and GET responses).\n\n5. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column so the persisted value round-trips on GET.\n\nThe test confirms the behavior: the first owner with a fresh surname gets `namesakeCount = 0`, the second gets `1`.",
